{
  "_doc": "How the world is planted. A rule is a species: how thick it grows, where it will stand, and what it grows along. `density` is instances per hectare before the region multiplier; `region_density` overrides that multiplier for one landform, because a wood is not a downland copse at a slightly higher density. `cluster` gates the species through a low-frequency field so it comes in copses and belts with open ground between, rather than an even sprinkle. `near_water`, `near_hedge` and `near_lane` are attraction bands [from_m, to_m, strength]: willows follow the watercourses, thorn follows the field boundaries, limes follow the lanes. Exclusion alone can only ever produce confetti. `tint_index` picks which of the region's six palette colours a species is painted from (-1 keeps the asset's own colour), and `tint_strength` how far from white that multiplier travels -- the forge already builds each asset in its region's palette, so a full-strength multiply paints the colour on twice, so a region's grass, its flowers and its heather are not all one wash. `pad_keep` is the fraction of a species that survives on the outer part of a settlement's pad, beyond `pad_from` (default 0.72) of its radius: the verge at the foot of the walls gets its rough grass back while the green and the streets stay clear. `tpi` [from_m, to_m, strength] is a band of height above the ground's own local mean (positive on a crest or a lip, negative in a hollow or a sunken street): marram on the crests of the strandplain, ash in the Builders' streets. `recipes` holds named patches the world build applies on request (`--recipe cover`): each is {\"dotted.path\": value} over these rules, and none is applied by default.",
